ell in  order to avoid a collapse or landslide  canada has been a world leader in organ  transplant surgery going back to 1983  when the first successful lung  transplant was performed in toronto  since then one toronto doctor has  devoted his life to lungs this week on  the new reality donna friesen introduces  us to the surgeon who's turning to the  skies in his quest to save more lives  donna  colleen dr khashoggi is one of the  world's top thoracic surgeons and it's  not just because of what he does on the  operating table he has spent his entire  life working on preserving lungs for  transport and this was a first a pair of  human lungs delivered by drone dr  kashabji tells me for him it was like  the kitty hawk and waiting in a hospital  bed was a retired engineer from the  ottawa area el hodak they came to the  room in the afternoon and they said  we've got this project that's  transporting by drones are you  interested in participating they  describe the risk of it  and i said  i'm a technical guy i'm an engineer by  profession  i said advancement of science sure i'll  participate dr khashoggi worked with a  montreal area company unifor  bioelectronics to develop the drone to  ensure the precious cargo would be  protected no matter what happened we  built in a ballistic parachute so with  two  ballistic parachutes right so if the if  the the drone drops too fast tilt  suddenly loses power it will cut all the  engines a parachute will fire and the  drone will come down to the ground  safely and it was very exciting because  like drone guys are like cool engineer  dudes and they can do all that stuff but  they didn't sort of think like it's a  line well i gave a drone crashes i'll  just get another one well no  it's got a lung in it that's a person  that's someone's life dr khashoggi has  made lung transplants his life work and  he's developed techniques to keep organs  alive outside the body preserving them  even tailoring them to the donor and all  his incredible innovations are happening  right here in canada  colleen  donna friesen thank you donna and you  can watch donna's full report tonight at  8 on the new reality right here on  global  next one students effort to reclaim  vancouver's black history  that's the queen kicking off the pomp  and ceremony to mark her platinum  jubilee tomorrow marks queen elizabeth  ii 70th anniversary on the throne the  longest reigning british monarch she  assumed the responsibility in 1952 at  the age of 25 when her father king  george vi died suddenly the queen who is  now 95 was in sparkling form today  laughing and joking with guests  a university of british columbia student  is spearheading an effort to create a  new community hub in an historic  neighborhood maya prashant wants to  establish the vancouver black library  she hopes the facility will reclaim some  of what was lost more than 50 years ago  paul johnson explains  hogan's alley was a very frequently  visited black neighborhood in canada  before the georgia viaduct was built  take a walk with maya preshon around the  neighborhood that once was hogan's alley  she can show you some of the landmarks  like the home on this block where jimi  hendrix's grandmother once lived and  where the guitar god frequently hung out  hogan's alley was really just a vibrant  community neighborhood the same way that  we have commercial drive as little italy  but while the drive chinatown and  vancouver's various other enclaves  mostly aged in place  the city's only black neighborhood was  torn down and dispersed to make way for  the georgia viaduct in 1970 and came  close to being erased from history as  well i am a black person in vancouver  and i was never taught about what  happened with hogan's alley so preshon  came up with an idea  why not create a library to gather all  the materials to tell the story of  hogan's alley and of the black  experience in canada  and also create the kind of community  hub that you see in other big cities  like toronto but not yet in vancouver a  community gathering space it's going to  be a place that people can study a place  where people can chill  preschon and her collaborators initially  set out to raise about six thousand  dollars but when their gofundme campaign  popped to seven grand way faster than  they thought they set their sights  higher  now hoping for 35 thousand dollars  enough to get a lease on a space and  hopefully become a magnet for materials  that go beyond the story of hogan's  alley we have a priority on  books literature  works that are written by black authors  put an emphasis and importance on the  black experience while those original  residents of hogan's alley may have  never expected their community would be  torn down  you also couldn't predict that fifty  years later someone like maya preschoon  would be working to put it back on the  map in a different way paul johnson  global news vancouver  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm colleen christie  tonight your canada is cultist lake near  chilliwack bc we love to see your corner  of the country so please keep sending  your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching i'll see you back  here tomorrow have a good night  [Music]  you
 

